  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><strong data-start=\"132\" data-end=\"142\">CPK-MM</strong> is the <strong data-start=\"150\" data-end=\"179\">muscle-specific isoenzyme</strong> of <strong data-start=\"183\" data-end=\"221\">Creatine Phosphokinase (CPK or CK)</strong>, and it is predominantly found in <strong data-start=\"256\" data-end=\"275\">skeletal muscle</strong>. Measuring CPK-MM is helpful in:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"310\" data-end=\"583\">\n<li data-start=\"310\" data-end=\"360\">\n<p data-start=\"312\" data-end=\"360\">Diagnosing <strong data-start=\"323\" data-end=\"360\">skeletal muscle damage or disease</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"361\" data-end=\"428\">\n<p data-start=\"363\" data-end=\"428\">Monitoring <strong data-start=\"374\" data-end=\"391\">muscle trauma</strong>, <strong data-start=\"393\" data-end=\"405\">myositis</strong>, or <strong data-start=\"410\" data-end=\"428\">rhabdomyolysis</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"429\" data-end=\"486\">\n<p data-start=\"431\" data-end=\"486\">Evaluating <strong data-start=\"442\" data-end=\"462\">muscle disorders</strong> like muscular dystrophy</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"487\" data-end=\"583\">\n<p data-start=\"489\" data-end=\"583\">Distinguishing between <strong data-start=\"512\" data-end=\"524\">skeletal</strong> and <strong data-start=\"529\" data-end=\"547\">cardiac muscle</strong> injury (in conjunction with CPK-MB)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Explanation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rhabdomyolysis</strong></td>\n<td>Severe muscle breakdown causes massive CPK-MM release into the blood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Muscular dystrophies (e.g., Duchenne)</strong></td>\n<td>Chronic muscle degeneration raises baseline CPK-MM levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Intense physical activity or trauma</strong></td>\n<td>Exercise, accidents, or muscle strain can lead to temporary elevation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Myositis or polymyositis</strong></td>\n<td>Inflammatory muscle diseases raise CPK-MM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Seizures or prolonged immobilization</strong></td>\n<td>Muscle stress from convulsions or pressure injuries may increase CPK-MM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Intramuscular injections or surgery</strong></td>\n<td>Iatrogenic muscle injury leads to mild to moderate elevation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hypothyroidism</strong></td>\n<td>Can cause muscle injury and raised CPK-MM levels</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
